From 17c01dae6af40a70ca7ff9afec0f7096a2bc9186 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Amir <hello@noury.ch>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 18:22:31 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 04/28] fix(ui): make Esc actually dismiss, and style the menu
 like a macOS menu
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Esc appeared not to work. It did set #hideSuggestions, but that flag is reset by
the very next ordinary keystroke, so the menu was suppressed for exactly one
character and then popped straight back.

- Adds #dismissed, which lasts for the rest of the command line. Lifted by Tab
  (the conventional 'complete this' request), by submitting, or once the line is
  empty. Deliberately NOT by up/down: upstream already uses those to keep the menu
  hidden while browsing shell history, so reusing them would give one key two
  contradictory meanings.
- The re-open check must NOT be gated on allowBindings: ui-root passes
  renderer.hasVisibleSuggestions, which is false precisely because the menu is
  dismissed, making the re-open key unreachable.
- Re-opening also needs an explicit scheduler request. ui-root only reloads when
  the command-state VERSION changes, and re-opening alters no screen content, so
  term.noop() left Tab dead — the menu only returned on the following keystroke.
  Surfaced via takeReopenRequest().

Styling now follows a native macOS menu: rounded corners (renderBox takes a
corners option), systemBlue #0A84FF selection, #3A3A3C hairline border, #98989D
secondary text, and no body fill so the terminal background shows through.
Row width stays a uniform 40 cells.

Verified in a real PTY: menu shows, Esc closes it, it stays closed across further
typing, and Tab brings it back immediately.
